+        "default": "pair=rotate_translate - the shipped case exactly. _pair_ops falls through to `_: return [_make_rotate_y(deg_to_rad(rotation_angle * t)), _make_translate(Vector3(translate_dist * t, 0, 0))]`, which is line for line the two transforms _redraw used to build inline, in the same roles (order A = first then second = rotate then translate; order B = the reverse). Every label formats back to its old literal at this value: the 36pt headline to 'R.T != T.R' (the relation is COMPUTED and rotate/translate do not commute at 45 degrees), the subtitle to 'Order matters in matrix multiplication', the legend to 'White = Original | Blue = R then T | Red = T then R', the two matrix captions to 'T.R (Rotate then Translate):' and 'R.T (Translate then Rotate):', and the info line to 'Rotation: 45 (Y-axis)   Translation: 0.30 (X-axis)'. All 6 placements are bare tokens (transform_composition:0:-0.5 in Trans_Introduction, Auto_/Germ_/GermAxis_/Script_Trans_Introduction, and Gallery_Transforms) passing no config, so none of them can reach another value. apply_grid_config also now returns before touching anything if it is called before _ready has built.",
+        "was": "two @export floats, rotation_angle and translate_dist, and both of them are MAGNITUDES - how far, how much - on an artifact whose entire claim is about ORDER. WHICH two operations were composed was welded into _redraw: _make_rotate_y and _make_translate called inline, with 'R.T != T.R' painted above them in 36 point. One case, standing in six maps, from which a walker cannot tell whether the != is a fact about composition or a fact about these two particular operators.",
+        "note": "rotate_scale is the value that pays for the axis, and it is the only one that argues against the title. A rotation about the origin and a UNIFORM scale about the origin commute - the scale's basis is a multiple of the identity - so the blue and red houses land in exactly the same place, the headline flips to 'R.S = S.R' in green and the subtitle reads 'These two commute'. That relation is MEASURED, not declared: _pair_commutes() composes both orders and asks is_equal_approx, so if a hand drops the rotation slider to zero it will honestly say '=' on the default pair too. The still is not empty at rotate_scale either, and this is the point to check before reading a small number as a dead axis: the two FINALS coincide but the two 0.25-alpha GHOSTS do not - rotated-only against scaled-only - which is the workbench's own line, that even a commuting pair takes two different roads to the same place.",
+        "kin": "the four pairs are transform_composition_workbench's four slider detents, same operations in the same order (rotate+translate, rotate+scale, translate+scale, rotate+rotate), so the two siblings walk the same evidence and should measure alike in kind. The workbench keeps them on a hand-turned slider - pair_index is its declared critical_parameter, not an axis - which means a still, a map and a placement have only ever met the first of the four. Here they are a placeable value. NOT sharing the workbench's `workings` (outcome | trace | operands | expression): that word asks how much of the computing goes on the table, and this artifact has exactly one piece of working to show or hide, the two ghosts, so it could only ever be a two-value axis whose 'outcome' state deletes the reason the ghosts are there. matrix_4x4_viewer's `pose` was checked and refused for the opposite reason: pose names ONE transform's kind for a console holding one matrix, and the claim here needs two and is about their product.",
+        "measurement_risk": "MEASURED SEPARATIONS, in world units, against a house 0.12 wide and reference axes 1.0 long, at the shipped 45 degrees / 0.30: rotate_translate finals 0.230 ghosts 0.320; translate_scale finals 0.090 ghosts 0.319; rotate_rotate finals 0.070 ghosts 0.103; rotate_scale finals 0.000 (that is the commuting proof, not a bug) ghosts 0.060. So the line drawing moves LESS at every non-default value than it does at the default, and rotate_scale's geometry barely moves at all - what carries that value is the 36pt headline flipping to '=' and turning green, the subtitle rewriting, and both 4x4 matrix blocks changing every cell. TWO THINGS TO CHECK BEFORE BELIEVING ANY NUMBER FROM THIS SWEEP. First, all six of those texts are Label3D, and the capture's AABB counts MeshInstance3D ONLY - the labels reach y=0.72 while the meshes sit around y=0.25 - so the pixels that change most may be framed out entirely; if the critic reports a low focus, confirm the headline and the matrix blocks were in frame before reading it as an inert axis. Second, _build_vr_controls adds a RackTemplates panel (two sliders, four buttons) at z=0.35 that is identical in every variant and is real geometry, so it both pulls the frame toward itself and dilutes whatever does change. No dna.framing is declared here, because setting one blind is the same error in the other direction - derive it from the first sweep's reported subject percentage.",
+        "declined": "an axis on which orders stand in the frame (both | first | second), from the _show_a/_show_b pair the A/B/A+B buttons already drive. It would photograph strongly - half the lines vanish - and that is exactly the trap. Both orders together is the only configuration in which this artifact's own furniture is honest: the headline says R.T != T.R and the legend names both colours, so a placement showing one order leaves the claim hanging over a picture that no longer shows it, and the critic would score the largest bite in the sweep on the value that argues least. Suppressing the headline along with it would be a second axis wearing the first one's coat.",
